data.content:
Done! Here's the summary:

**What changed:** `InvoiceCalculator` now accepts an optional `TimeProvider` parameter (defaults to `TimeProvider.System`, so existing callers are unaffected).

**In your tests**, use `FakeTimeProvider` from `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing`:

```csharp
// Add NuGet: Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing (to test project only)
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-365));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest approach:**
- `TimeProvider` is built into .NET 8+ — no custom wrapper needed
- The optional parameter means zero breaking changes for existing callers
- No DI container required
